        "id": "s1_androecium",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Androecium",
        "marks": 1,
        "lines": [
          "The essential organs are the androecium and the",
          "gynoecium.",
          "Androecium:",
          "Five stamens, epipetalous — the filaments are",
          "joined to the corolla tube — and they alternate",
          "with the petals.",
          "Anthers are dithecous, basifixed and introrse."
        ],
        "why": "Epipetalous and alternating with the petals fixes both where the stamens are attached and where they sit, which is what a technical description needs.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing epiphyllous. That word is for a perianth; Solanaceae has a corolla, so the word is epipetalous.",
          "Writing that the stamens are opposite the petals. They alternate with them."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Solanaceae counts in fives: five sepals, five petals, five stamens. Only the carpels are different, at two.",
        "margin_note": "Head the block \"Androecium\". The word epipetalous must appear.",
        "mark_note": "Androecium"
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_gynoecium",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Gynoecium",
        "marks": 1,
        "lines": [
          "Gynoecium:",
          "Bicarpellary and syncarpous, with a bilocular",
          "superior ovary.",
          "Placentation is axile and the placenta is swollen.",
          "The style is terminal and the stigma is capitate."
        ],
        "why": "Two fused carpels give two chambers with a central column, and the swollen placenta filling those chambers is a Solanaceae feature.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing trilocular. That is Liliaceae.",
          "Leaving out the swollen placenta, which the book marks separately."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Two carpels, two locules, one swollen placenta in the middle.",
        "margin_note": "Head the block \"Gynoecium\". The swollen placenta is a separate fact.",
        "mark_note": "Gynoecium"
